[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Configure and operate the DMS (/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/dms)\n\n\n\nA **Device Management Service (DMS)** configures admission and identity lifecycle for its devices. It acts as a registration authority; the CA selected in `Enrollment CA` signs certificates. This guide is for the policy administrator.\n\nBefore you begin [#before-you-begin]\n\nYou need DMS management permissions, an active CA with a usable key and a tested [issuance profile](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-profiles). Agree device ID, initial credential, consumer and renewal window with the integrator. Review [Provisioning strategies](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/provisioning-strategies) before choosing registration and replacement.\n\nPrepare EST server trust and device connectivity through [Deployment](/docs/deployment/self-hosted/networking). Your operator’s administrative authentication does not replace device EST authentication.\n\nConfigure a DMS [#configure-a-dms]\n\n<Steps>\n  <Step>\n    Identify the fleet [#identify-the-fleet]\n\n    Create the DMS with a recognizable name and retain its ID. EST URLs use this identifier, not the display name. Each device retains one owning DMS.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Select the enrollment CA [#select-the-enrollment-ca]\n\n    Choose **Enrollment CA** and an explicit profile appropriate for the end-entity identity. Keep CN compatible with `Device ID`. The test certificate should allow its intended purpose and expire within issuer validity.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Configure EST [#configure-est]\n\n    Select initial enrollment registration and authentication. Separately configure re-enrollment authentication, thresholds and previous certificate retirement. Leave ServerKeyGen disabled if the client generates its key.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Distribute trust [#distribute-trust]\n\n    Select certificates returned by `/cacerts`. Explicitly include required intermediates and roots, keeping distribution distinct from automatic trust installation.\n  </Step>\n\n  <Step>\n    Verify the endpoints [#verify-the-endpoints]\n\n    Save and read configuration again. Query `/cacerts`, perform an allowed and a rejected enrollment, and inspect device, serial and history. Creating the DMS or downloading cacerts does not establish working authentication and issuance.\n  </Step>\n</Steps>\n\nEnrollment configuration [#enrollment-configuration]\n\nEnrollment Device Registration [#enrollment-device-registration]\n\n`settings.enrollment_settings.registration_mode` accepts `JITP` or `PRE_REGISTRATION`. JITP creates an unknown device with the CSR CN as ID and the DMS as owner. `device_provisioning_profile` supplies tags, metadata and icon for that registration.\n\nPRE\\_REGISTRATION requires an existing ID. In the inspected code, an existing device encounters replacement checks first, even without an identity. Review [preregistration conditions](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/provisioning-strategies#preregistration-and-replacement) before using this mode.\n\nEnrollment Settings [#enrollment-settings]\n\n| `settings.enrollment_settings` field | Decision                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `protocol`                           | `EST_RFC7030` for these operations.                                                                                                                        |\n| `enrollment_ca`                      | ID of the signing CA.                                                                                                                                      |\n| `verify_csr_signature`               | Keep `true` to verify CSR key possession.                                                                                                                  |\n| `enable_replaceable_enrollment`      | Allows `/simpleenroll` for an existing ID in the same DMS. It can attempt previous certificate revocation even if a later phase fails; this is not atomic. |\n| `est_rfc7030_settings.auth_mode`     | One of the four authentication modes.                                                                                                                      |\n\nInitial enrollment rejects an ID owned by another DMS. Do not automatically enable replacement in response to an error; [Troubleshooting](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/troubleshooting) distinguishes repeated IDs, ownership and partial changes.\n\nAuthentication per operation [#authentication-per-operation]\n\nThe blocks are independent:\n\n* `settings.enrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings` for `/simpleenroll` and `/serverkeygen`.\n* `settings.reenrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings` for `/simplereenroll`.\n\n| `auth_mode`                               | Control applied in the configured operation          |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE`                      | Presented certificate validation.                    |\n| `EXTERNAL_WEBHOOK`                        | Configured webhook authorization.                    |\n| `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_AND_EXTERNAL_WEBHOOK` | Certificate validation followed by webhook approval. |\n| `NO_AUTH`                                 | No DMS requester authentication.                     |\n\nWebhook and combined modes also execute during re-enrollment when configured in its block. They do not implicitly degrade to NO\\_AUTH. An absent or unrecognized mode fails.\n\nFor initial mTLS, `client_certificate_settings` contains `validation_cas`, `allow_expired` and `chain_level_validation`. The inspected flow receives one certificate credential; it does not guarantee forwarding every handshake intermediate to the validator. For a direct case, configure the bootstrap’s direct issuer as validation CA and test the actual Gateway path.\n\nFor certificate re-enrollment, the validator tries the enrollment CA first and then the CAs listed in `reenrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings.client_certificate_settings.validation_cas`; those are the re-enrollment validation CAs. That list is optional because the enrollment CA is always trusted for re-enrollment, and each settings block consults only its own `validation_cas`. CAs listed only in the initial block's `validation_cas` do not extend re-enrollment acceptance; during a CA rotation, add the old issuance CA to the re-enrollment block's list. The re-enrollment validator compares the presented TLS leaf against those CAs; the re-enrollment block's `chain_level_validation` and `allow_expired` values are stored but not consulted, and expiry is governed by `enable_expired_renewal`.\n\nFor a webhook, configure destination, Lamassu-to-service authentication, method and timeout. The [authentication contract](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/bootstrap-identity) explains the device credential and request body. `NO_AUTH` does not establish ownership of the requested ID.\n\nExternal webhook settings [#external-webhook-settings]\n\nThe webhook authentication modes read `external_webhook_settings` in the same `est_rfc7030_settings` block:\n\n| `external_webhook_settings` field | Decision                                                                                                                                         |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `url`                             | Absolute `http` or `https` URL of the receiver. It must be the final URL; redirects are not followed.                                            |\n| `method`                          | `POST` or `PUT`; `POST` by default.                                                                                                              |\n| `name`                            | Label used in service logs.                                                                                                                      |\n| `config.auth_mode`                | `noauth`, `apikey`, `jwt` or `mtls`. An absent or unknown value behaves as `noauth`.                                                             |\n| `config.log_level`                | Log level of the webhook call: `debug`, `info`, `warn` or `error`; `info` by default.                                                            |\n| `config.apikey_options`           | Required for `apikey`: `header` and `key`. Reserved header names are rejected.                                                                   |\n| `config.jwt_options`              | Required for `jwt`: `oidc_client_id`, `oidc_client_secret` and an absolute `http` or `https` `oidc_well_known`.                                  |\n| `config.mtls_options`             | Required for `mtls`: `cert_content` and `key_content` as Base64-encoded PEM, like certificates elsewhere in the API; plain PEM is also accepted. |\n| `config.insecure_skip_verify`     | Skips verification of the receiver certificate when `true`. Verification is on by default. Unlike alert channels, this flag is honored here.     |\n| `config.call_timeout`             | Maximum duration of the authorization call (for example `10s`); 10 seconds by default.                                                           |\n\nRules for that configuration:\n\n* Creating or updating a DMS whose enrollment or reenrollment `auth_mode` uses the webhook validates the block and returns HTTP 400 — the body starts with `struct validation error:` and continues with `invalid enrollment external webhook config` or `invalid reenrollment external webhook config` — when the URL, method or options are invalid. That includes an `mtls_options.cert_content`/`key_content` pair that does not decode to a matching certificate and private key. File references (`ca_cert_file`, `mtls_options.cert_file`, `mtls_options.key_file`) are always invalid: the service does not read files, so enter the certificate and key as Base64-encoded PEM in `mtls_options.cert_content` and `key_content`, or as plain PEM.\n* A custom `apikey_options.header` may not use names that the platform or the receiving service trusts — for example `X-Principal-Id`, `X-Request-Id`, `Host`, `Content-Type` or `Forwarded` — and no name may start with `X-Forwarded-`, `X-Envoy-` or `X-Lms-` (case-insensitive). The [DMS API reference](/docs/api-reference/dms-manager) documents the `WebhookCall` and `WebhookCallHttpClient` schemas and that reserved API-key headers are rejected.\n* The nested `http_client` key exists only in alert channel configurations; in a DMS body it is ignored. The DMS key is `external_webhook_settings.config`.\n* A body with `config.auth_mode` set to `apikey`, `jwt` or `mtls` must carry its corresponding option block (`apikey_options`, `jwt_options` or `mtls_options`) — and nothing else. Bodies with those auth modes carrying only `apikey`, `oidc` or `mtls` fail validation with 400, and `validate_server_cert` is always ignored: use `insecure_skip_verify`. Only bodies without option blocks are accepted as `noauth`, and their credentials are dropped.\n* The authorization response must be JSON of at most 1 MiB; a larger body fails the call.\n\n<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"Credentials over http://\">\n  With credentials configured, they are sent regardless of the URL scheme: an `http://` webhook receives the API key or bearer token in cleartext, and mTLS has no effect because no TLS handshake takes place, so the client certificate is never presented. Prefer `https://` for `url`, `oidc_well_known` and the token endpoint it advertises.\n</Callout>\n\nInitial authentication and renewal settings are stored independently. If a DMS only has the initial authentication block, renewal reuses it: read both blocks before editing. The console exposes independent editors for the enrollment and re-enrollment authentication, with the same Log Level, Call Timeout, HTTP Authentication and credential fields as the webhook channels, plus a **Skip TLS Verification** switch (off by default, so verification stays on; the console warns when you enable it).\n\nWebhook credentials must be stored as PEM content. File references (`ca_cert_file`, `mtls_options.cert_file`, `mtls_options.key_file`) are never valid in a stored webhook config: delivery rejects any webhook config that references files, whatever its auth mode, so every enroll and reenroll that calls that webhook fails until the certificate and key are entered as Base64-encoded or plain PEM in `mtls_options.cert_content` and `key_content`, or the file fields are removed if the webhook does not use mTLS. If a stored config holds file paths in `mtls_options.cert_content` or `key_content`, every full settings update (`POST /v1/dms`, `PUT /v1/dms/:id`) fails validation — settings are replaced wholesale, so the stored config must be resent — until the values are replaced by PEM content or the webhook stops being used. Meanwhile, delivery of that webhook fails on every call; a metadata-only update through `PUT` or `PATCH` on `/v1/dms/:id/metadata` still succeeds. To fix it in the console, open the DMS, replace any file-path value with PEM content — the editor rejects file paths with an inline field error — and save. Saving rewrites `external_webhook_settings.config` with the current keys, and the console reads both plain and Base64-encoded PEM from migrated configurations and stores the material Base64-encoded. If your stored config previously relied on `validate_server_cert: false`, it now carries `insecure_skip_verify: true`: receiver-certificate verification stays skipped until you set that flag to `false`. See [3.8.0 to 4.0.0](/docs/upgrades/3-8-0-to-4-0-0) for the upgrade notes and the operator checklist.\n\nThe re-enrollment trust set lives in `reenrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings.client_certificate_settings.validation_cas`. The flat field `reenrollment_settings.additional_validation_cas` is not part of the API: bodies that carry it are accepted but the value is silently ignored — unknown JSON fields are dropped — and GET responses do not include it. Read the DMS before sending API edits: an update body built with that flat field silently empties `validation_cas`.\n\nIssuance profile [#issuance-profile]\n\n`settings.issuance_profile_id` takes precedence over `settings.issuance_profile`; without either, the CA default profile is used. Rules are documented once in [Profiles](/docs/platform/pki/certificate-profiles). DMS changes affect future requests; they do not modify signed certificates or install client trust.\n\nRe-enrollment configuration [#re-enrollment-configuration]\n\n| `settings.reenrollment_settings` field                            | Purpose                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `reenrollment_delta`                                              | Opens the window at the active certificate’s `NotAfter` minus this delta.                                                                                                                                              |\n| `preventive_delta`                                                | Preventive operational monitoring threshold.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `critical_delta`                                                  | Critical operational monitoring threshold.                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `enable_expired_renewal`                                          | Accepts an expired presented certificate in the renewal mTLS validator. Does not authorize another mode or remove Gateway constraints by itself.                                                                       |\n| `est_rfc7030_settings.client_certificate_settings.validation_cas` | Re-enrollment validation CAs for the renewal mTLS credential, tried after the enrollment CA; optional, because the enrollment CA is always trusted. Typically populated during CA rotation while old-CA cohorts renew. |\n| `revoke_on_reenrollment`                                          | If the previous certificate is ACTIVE, attempts Superseded revocation before binding its successor.                                                                                                                    |\n\nMonitoring does not renew: the client must schedule the request and installation. Webhook and NO\\_AUTH modes do not use the same mTLS expired-credential validator; the webhook must explicitly authorize permitted recovery. Review [Renewal and recovery](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/renewal-and-recovery) for operation order and partial failures.\n\nServerKeyGen [#serverkeygen]\n\n`settings.server_keygen_settings.enabled` enables software key generation on the server. The console exposes it through an **Enable server-side key generation** switch and a **Key type** selection: `ECDSA` P-256, P-384 or P-521, or `RSA` with 2048, 3072 or 4096 bits; the defaults are P-256 for `ECDSA` and 4096 bit for `RSA`. Algorithm and size come from its `key` field, stored as `{type,bits}` where P-256, P-384 and P-521 map to `bits` 256, 384 and 521; `key` is omitted when disabled. The profile must permit them. The response contains an unencrypted PKCS#8 key inside TLS alongside the certificate. See format and extraction in [EST reference](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-reference#serverkeygen).\n\nTrust distribution [#trust-distribution]\n\n| Option                        | Published material                                                         |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Include Lamassu System CA** | Available downstream TLS certificate; it can be a leaf, not an issuing CA. |\n| **Include Enrollment CA**     | Selected issuing CA certificate.                                           |\n| **Managed CAs**               | Explicitly selected CA certificates.                                       |\n\nAll ancestors are not added automatically. [Trust distribution](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/trust-distribution) distinguishes trusted roots, intermediates and initial server trust.\n\nOperate the DMS inventory [#operate-the-dms-inventory]\n\n**Edit** changes name, settings and metadata. Backend updates replace these complete blocks; read the DMS before sending API edits to avoid losing fields.\n\n**Go to DMS owned devices** restricts inventory by owner. **Show/Edit Metadata** contains operational and integration data; consult the integration guide before changing it. **EST (RFC-7030)** helps locate URLs.\n\n**Delete** removes the DMS record and configuration; it does not itself cascade revocation across every device and certificate. Migrate dependencies and retain audit references before retirement.\n\nIntegrate the devices [#integrate-the-devices]\n\nContinue with [EST client](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-client) or the [enrollment and mTLS tutorial](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/quickstarts/enroll-device). [EST reference](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/est-reference) covers formats; [Troubleshooting](/docs/platform/iot-fleets/enrollment/troubleshooting) covers failures.\n"]
